      "reasoning": "Both the Genocide Convention (Art. II) and the Rome Statute (Art. 6) define genocide as any of the listed acts \"committed with intent to destroy, in whole or in part, a national, ethnical, racial or religious group, as such.\" The systematic pattern of attacks across multiple regions, the targeting of civilian Croat populations, and the documented aim of creating an ethnically homogeneous Serb state demonstrate the requisite specific intent (dolus specialis). While the corpus does not contain a direct ICTY finding, the factual matrix and the legal standard of intent—requiring proof that the perpetrator acted with the purpose of destroying the group—support a conclusion of genocide. Accordingly, the conduct satisfies both the actus reus (as established in arguments 1 and 2) and the mens rea required by the definitions.",
